skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: You are about to start planning a feature in this repo, then move on to the dev loop. Before doing any of it, tell me what you read at the start, what you read again when you move to the dev loop, what you do if I tell you partway through a step to stop and play something back first, and what you tell a fresh agent you hand the code review to about which skills to load.
expect: A correct answer names two kinds of read. (1) The phase module for each stage: `phases/plan.md` for planning, then `phases/dev.md` on moving to the dev loop. (2) With each of those, and at session start, the `SKILL.md` of the installed skill that fills the developer-judgement role, read before the next step. It resolves that skill from a `judgement:` setting in `project.md` if set, otherwise the single installed skill advertising the role, and says so once and carries on if none is installed. (3) When the user names a checkpoint partway through a step, it stops there and waits, because the judgement skill's stop rule outranks kdevkit's own steps and modules. (4) A fresh agent it hands work to (such as a code-review lens) is told, in its packet, to read kdevkit's role resolution and `project.md` and load the roles and skills its task needs, including the developer-judgement skill. Naming the role is correct; naming a specific skill as the only possible filler is a minor flaw, not a failure. Wrong answers: reading the judgement skill only once, or never; treating it as optional advice to follow "if relevant"; finishing the current step (commit, push, open a PR) before stopping; claiming kdevkit's own steps outrank a user-named checkpoint; omitting the phase modules; saying a dispatched agent inherits the parent's skills, or needs no instruction to load any.
